  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Friday...\n\nEarly morning cold front will finally rid us of the hot & dry air\nmass we have dealt with for most of the past week. Still need to\nwatch for periodic downslope gusts along eastern slopes of the \nmountains until the fropa, which should occur over the southern \nfoothills in the 12-15z time frame. Post-frontal N-E winds will \nbring a much cooler day with temps in the 50s in the NW to mid 70s\nin the S/SE. Cold advection increases again this evening and wind\ngusts will be stronger, as temps fall to the 30s to lower 40s. \nShower potential increases over the west today, and cannot rule \nout a few weak t-storms in the south in the afternoon, otherwise \nthe main show begins tonight as an upper low drops thru the PacNW \nand a very moist SW flow overruns the deepening cool air on our \nside of the mountains.\n\nAs confidence has increased for accumulating snow over western \nmountains and foothills have already issued winter highlights for\nthese areas. Confidence has also increased that eastern zones,\nespecially Rosebud County eastward, will remain too warm for\nanything but rain, with lower precip amounts overall than further\nwest. These trends are due to the fact that the PacNW low will \ntrack southward near the coast. Not only does this keep the \nboundary layer over the east warmer (strongest pressure rises/cold\nadvection drops from Canada thru central MT), but the track of \nmoisture from former hurricane Rachel will track thru central MT \nrather than be shunted to the east. Precip forecast has been \nadjusted accordingly, with 1-2\" west of Billings (greatest over \nmountains), 0.40-0.80\" across the northeast, and only 0.10-0.40\" \nfor the southeast.\n\nSurge of moisture from Rachel is expected to push pwats to near an\ninch, remarkable for October and 200% or more of normal. If the\nair mass were slightly colder we'd be looking at a major winter \nstorm, but as it is the surface temps will hold back snow \naccumulations quite a bit, especially during the daytime hours. \nThe toughest forecast at this time seems to be in the area east of\nthe western foothills and west of Rosebud County, where rain \nshould be dominant and any snow accumulation will be elevation \ndependent and may be limited to only the higher hills. That said,\nthe moisture surge Sunday night into Monday may be the greatest \nopportunity for this area to see wet snowfall, and this includes \nthe city of Billings. The chance of an inch of snow at Billings \nremains low at 20% but continue to stress the uncertainty. The \nrisk of at least a foot of snow over the western mountains is \n60-80% above 8kft. This is an interesting setup as even the \nBighorns will struggle to see snowfall with snow levels of 9-10kft\nacross N/NE Wyoming.\n\nThere is emerging strong consensus that once the Rachel moisture \nexits Monday the western low will cut off and we will see drying. \nMonday will remain chilly and there is a high risk of a freeze \nover much of the region Monday night (for those areas that remain \ntoo warm during the system itself).\n\nAfter chilly days Sunday & Monday (highs in mid 30s to mid 40s), \nTuesday should be dry and warmer with temps back to the 50s. The \ncut off low over the SW is expected to lift to the northeast and \nbring mainly the east a chance of rain showers Wednesday. We'll \nhave to see about a Canadian clipper into Thursday as all models \nhave trended weaker, but there is a low chance of rain/snow \nshowers in the east and southern upslope areas, along with breezy \nNW winds. Friday is looking quiet, but perhaps still cool if the \nupper ridge axis stays to our west for another day. Generally \nspeaking look for high temps in the 50s to lower 60s through the \n2nd half of the work week.\n\nLooking further ahead, there is growing confidence for a building\nridge and return of warmer than normal temps next weekend.\n\nJKL",
